window.unit1Data = {"topicNumber": 1, "topicName": "Global Sustainability", "sections": [{"sectionTitle": "Sustainability in the contemporary world", "dotpoints": [{"number": 1, "text": "Sustainability and sustainable development, including pillars of sustainability \u2013 social, economic, environmental and cultural", "notes": ["Sustainable development = development that meets the needs of the current generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their needs.", "Sustainable development integrates economic growth, environmental stewardship, and social equity, aiming for a balanced approach to progress.", "In the 21st century, sustainability is crucial for tackling pressing global issues such as climate change, resource depletion, and social inequality.", "Four pillars of sustainability (diagram: pillars hold up a 'Sustainability' roof) are Social, Economic, Environmental and Cultural.", "Social sustainability: equity, diversity, quality of life; access to education, healthcare, housing and safety; focus on equity, inclusion and wellbeing now and in the future.", "Economic sustainability: long-term financial stability and growth without depleting resources; uses cost-benefit analysis (CBA) to assess project viability; promotes fair employment and responsible production/investment (e.g. tourism).", "Environmental sustainability: preserves natural resources (recycling, tree planting), protects biodiversity, reduces pollution and uses resources wisely; key concept is 'natural capital' \u2014 the world's stocks of natural assets (geology, soil, air, water, living things); initiatives include renewable energy such as wind turbines and solar panels.", "Cultural sustainability: preserves and promotes cultural heritage, practices and values for future generations; supports social cohesion and identity; transmission of languages, traditions and cultural practices between generations; respects and preserves the identity of communities, especially Indigenous peoples.", "The UN Sustainable Development Goals (UNSDGs) consist of 17 goals, established in 2015, promoting prosperity while protecting the planet, with a target for achievement by 2030."], "caseStudies": [], "data": ["17 UN Sustainable Development Goals, established 2015, target year 2030"], "flashcards": [{"term": "Sustainable development", "definition": "Development that meets the needs of the current generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs."}, {"term": "Four pillars of sustainability", "definition": "Social, economic, environmental and cultural sustainability \u2014 the four interdependent dimensions that together support overall sustainability."}, {"term": "Natural capital", "definition": "The world's stocks of natural assets, including geology, soil, air, water, and all living things, which provide resources and services to society."}, {"term": "Cost-benefit analysis (CBA)", "definition": "A method used in economic sustainability to assess the viability of a project by weighing its costs against its benefits, aiming for efficiency and reduced waste."}, {"term": "Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)", "definition": "17 goals established by the UN in 2015 as a universal call to action to promote prosperity while protecting the planet, targeted for achievement by 2030."}]}, {"number": 2, "text": "Principles of ecologically sustainable development \u2013 precautionary principle, intergenerational equity, conservation of biological diversity and ecological integrity", "notes": ["Ecologically Sustainable Development (ESD) is a framework that seeks to balance economic growth, environmental protection, and social equity.", "Intergenerational equity is the concept of fairness or justice between different generations, concerned with how today's actions affect the environment and resources available to future generations; in economic terms it means not burdening future generations with excessive debt or depleted resources.", "The precautionary principle is a strategy for approaching issues of potential harm when extensive scientific knowledge is lacking; it emphasises caution, pausing and review before adopting new innovations that may prove disastrous.", "The precautionary principle has three key components: (1) preventive action \u2014 acting to prevent harm even where cause-and-effect is not fully scientifically established; (2) shifting the burden of proof \u2014 those proposing an action must prove it is not harmful, rather than opponents proving it is; (3) public participation \u2014 involving the public in decision-making to ensure transparency and inclusivity.", "Conservation of biological diversity refers to efforts and strategies aimed at protecting and preserving the variety of life forms on Earth, including preserving species diversity (plants, animals, microorganisms) and protecting ecosystems that provide vital services.", "Ecological integrity refers to the ability of an ecosystem to support and maintain a community of organisms \u2014 essentially that the ecosystem is healthy, stable and functioning as it should, without significant human-induced disruption."], "caseStudies": [], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Ecologically Sustainable Development (ESD)", "definition": "A framework that seeks to balance economic growth, environmental protection, and social equity."}, {"term": "Intergenerational equity", "definition": "Fairness between generations \u2014 ensuring that today's actions do not burden future generations with excessive debt or depleted resources."}, {"term": "Precautionary principle", "definition": "A strategy of caution when scientific knowledge is incomplete, involving preventive action, shifting the burden of proof onto those proposing an action, and public participation in decision-making."}, {"term": "Conservation of biological diversity", "definition": "Efforts and strategies to protect and preserve the variety of life forms on Earth, including species diversity and ecosystem health."}, {"term": "Ecological integrity", "definition": "The ability of an ecosystem to support and maintain a community of organisms in a healthy, stable, functioning state without significant human-induced disruption."}]}, {"number": 3, "text": "Opportunities and challenges in planning for and achieving global sustainability, including the role of global forums, agreements and cooperation; scaled action from SDGs to local (Global, IGO, NGO, corporations, community, individuals); Indigenous Peoples' practices and benefit sharing; PETSCE influences", "notes": ["Global forums are platforms where international issues are discussed and solutions negotiated among global leaders.", "The UNFCCC (United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change) is an international environmental treaty established in 1992 at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, aiming to stabilise greenhouse gas concentrations to prevent dangerous human interference with the climate system; it recognises 'common but differentiated responsibilities' and led to the Kyoto Protocol (1997) and Paris Agreement (2015); it holds annual Conference of the Parties (COP) meetings.", "The Paris Agreement (2015), adopted under the UNFCCC, aims to limit global warming to well below 2\u00b0C, preferably 1.5\u00b0C, above pre-industrial levels; countries commit to Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) updated every five years; it is non-binding in enforcement, relying on peer pressure and public accountability.", "Cooperation is essential for sustainable development because it enables countries and communities to share resources, knowledge, and solutions to tackle global challenges \u2014 'global problems require global solutions'.", "Levels of action occur at a range of scales: the UN 2030 Agenda (17 SDGs + 169 targets) at the global scale; IGOs such as the IPCC; NGOs such as WaterAid Australia; corporations such as Air New Zealand, Tesla and IKEA; community organisations such as Community Greening, Sydney Alliance and Randwick City Council volunteer groups; and individual actions.", "Global-scale opportunities include shared goals and coordination, resource/knowledge sharing, political commitment and accountability, economic growth in green industries, and global awareness and cooperation.", "Global-scale challenges include conflicting national interests, weak enforcement and compliance, high costs (especially for developing nations), political instability and short-term priorities, difficult coordination, and equity issues over who pays/acts.", "Community-level opportunities include community-led solutions, sharing local knowledge, strengthened social cohesion, economic benefits, and awareness/education; challenges include limited funding, conflicting local priorities, lack of expertise, resistance to change, and coordination with wider government policy.", "Individual actions include buying from local/regenerative producers (e.g. Paddock to Plate, 100 Food Miles), eating less meat (Veganuary, Meat-Free Mondays), composting, engaging with the circular economy, and sharing social media posts promoting the UNSDGs.", "Broader opportunities for global sustainability include technological advancements, international cooperation through global forums, the green economy, community engagement/grassroots movements, education and awareness campaigns, and investment in sustainable infrastructure.", "Broader challenges include rapid population growth and resource consumption, climate change risks, economic disparities between nations, political obstacles/lack of consensus, difficulty balancing growth with protection, and insufficient public awareness.", "Indigenous peoples have practised sustainable resource management for millennia through Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK), including cultural burning, which manages landscapes, reduces catastrophic wildfire risk, nourishes soil with ash, clears weeds and assists germination.", "Principles of Indigenous land management include: only use what you need; do not waste anything; move on (nomadic use) so an area is not overhunted/fished/harvested; and ensure a steady, long-term supply of food.", "Integrating TEK into modern environmental management can enhance conservation efforts and benefit sharing; Indigenous knowledge supports UN SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and SDG 15 (Life on Land).", "'Benefit sharing' is the fair and equitable distribution of social, economic and environmental advantages arising from the use of natural resources, ecosystems, or cultural knowledge; protecting intellectual property rights safeguards traditional knowledge from exploitation.", "PETSCE influences shaping sustainability: Political (environmental laws, funding, cooperation between nations), Economic (resource use, production, access to sustainable technologies \u2014 see Environmental Kuznets Curve), Technological (advances that can support or accelerate degradation, e.g. solar power, GIS), Social (education and inequality affecting engagement), Cultural (values affecting attitudes to conservation, land use and development), and Environmental (conditions setting limits for sustainable growth, e.g. resource endowment and climate).", "The Environmental Kuznets Curve shows environmental degradation rising then falling with income, peaking at middle-income levels, moving from a 'scale effect' to a 'composition and technique effect' across pre-industrial, industrial and post-industrial economy stages."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "UNFCCC and Paris Agreement", "facts": ["UNFCCC established 1992 at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro; aims to stabilise greenhouse gas concentrations.", "Recognises 'common but differentiated responsibilities' \u2014 developed nations expected to lead on emissions reductions.", "Led to the Kyoto Protocol (1997) and the Paris Agreement (2015); holds annual COP meetings (e.g. COP21 Paris, COP28 Dubai).", "Paris Agreement's central goal: limit warming to well below 2\u00b0C, preferably 1.5\u00b0C, above pre-industrial levels.", "Countries submit Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs), updated every five years; the agreement is non-binding, relying on peer pressure and public accountability."]}, {"name": "IPCC (Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change) \u2014 IGO action", "facts": ["Provides regular assessments of the scientific basis of climate change, its impacts, future risks, and adaptation/mitigation options.", "Gives governments scientific information to develop climate policies and is a key input into international climate negotiations.", "Currently has 195 member countries; thousands of contributors worldwide."]}, {"name": "WaterAid Australia \u2014 NGO action", "facts": ["Mission: 'Clean water, decent toilets and good hygiene are vital for living a dignified, healthy life.'", "Convinces governments to change laws, links policy makers with people on the ground, changes attitudes and behaviours, pools knowledge and resources, and rallies international support."]}, {"name": "Corporate action \u2014 Air New Zealand, Tesla, IKEA", "facts": ["Air New Zealand trials sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) and electric aircraft; focuses on carbon offsetting and biodiversity protection.", "Tesla innovates in clean technology, producing electric vehicles and solar energy products, with gigafactories designed for energy efficiency and minimal waste.", "IKEA pursues a circular economy and resource efficiency, aims to be climate positive by 2030, offers furniture take-back/recycling, and invests in renewable energy and sustainable forestry."]}, {"name": "Community organisations \u2014 Community Greening, Sydney Alliance, Randwick City Council volunteers", "facts": ["Community Greening (Botanic Gardens of Sydney): non-profit outreach empowering communities through gardening projects, promoting environmental sustainability, health and social connection, especially in disadvantaged areas.", "Sydney Alliance: coalition of community groups, unions and faith organisations working for a fair, just and sustainable Sydney, campaigning on energy, housing and Indigenous rights.", "Randwick City Council Sustainable Community Volunteers (e.g. ACF Eastern Sydney, BIKEast, Climate Action Sydney Eastern Suburbs) run local initiatives such as bush regeneration, cycling advocacy and climate awareness."]}, {"name": "Stockton Sand Dunes / Worimi Conservation Lands (NSW) \u2014 Indigenous benefit sharing", "facts": ["The Worimi people are recognised as custodians of the Worimi Conservation Lands.", "They have majority representation in land management decisions.", "They receive cultural and economic benefits through conservation, employment, and tourism opportunities."]}], "data": ["17 SDGs + 169 targets guide countries in integrating the four pillars, with a 2030 target", "IPCC has 195 member countries", "UNFCCC established 1992; Kyoto Protocol 1997; Paris Agreement 2015", "Paris Agreement goal: limit warming to well below 2\u00b0C, preferably 1.5\u00b0C, above pre-industrial levels"], "flashcards": [{"term": "UNFCCC", "definition": "United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change \u2014 an international treaty established in 1992 at the Rio Earth Summit to stabilise greenhouse gas concentrations and prevent dangerous climate interference."}, {"term": "Paris Agreement", "definition": "A 2015 international agreement under the UNFCCC aiming to limit global warming to well below 2\u00b0C (preferably 1.5\u00b0C), using non-binding Nationally Determined Contributions updated every five years."}, {"term": "Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK)", "definition": "Ancestral Indigenous knowledge for sustainably managing land and resources, including practices like cultural burning, passed down through generations."}, {"term": "Cultural burning", "definition": "An Indigenous land management practice that reduces the risk of catastrophic wildfire, nourishes soil with ash, clears weeds, and assists germination of new plants."}, {"term": "Benefit sharing", "definition": "The fair and equitable distribution of social, economic, and environmental advantages arising from the use of natural resources, ecosystems, or cultural knowledge, as seen at the Worimi Conservation Lands."}, {"term": "Environmental Kuznets Curve", "definition": "A model showing environmental degradation rising then falling as income grows, peaking at middle-income levels, reflecting a shift from a 'scale effect' to a 'composition and technique effect' across development stages."}]}]}, {"sectionTitle": "Evaluating sustainability", "dotpoints": [{"number": 4, "text": "The reasons for evaluating and monitoring global sustainability", "notes": ["Tracking progress: evaluating sustainability enables tracking of progress toward sustainability goals, allowing timely adjustments and improvements in policies and practices.", "Identifying challenges: regular monitoring helps identify challenges and areas where progress is lagging, making targeted interventions possible.", "Informing policy: evaluation provides data that supports the development of more effective and efficient regulations and initiatives.", "Enhancing accountability: sustainability evaluations ensure governments, corporations, and other stakeholders uphold their commitments, fostering transparency and trust.", "Driving innovation: by highlighting gaps and challenges, evaluations encourage the creation of new technologies and practices that contribute to a more sustainable future.", "Supporting investment decisions: sustainability metrics support investment decisions, as investors increasingly favour companies that demonstrate strong sustainability performance, viewing them as lower risk and more resilient long-term."], "caseStudies": [], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Track progress", "definition": "Evaluating sustainability enables progress toward sustainability goals to be tracked, allowing timely adjustments to policies and practices."}, {"term": "Identify challenges", "definition": "Regular monitoring helps identify challenges and areas where progress is lagging, making targeted interventions possible."}, {"term": "Inform policy", "definition": "Evaluation provides data that supports the development of more effective and efficient regulations and initiatives."}, {"term": "Enhance accountability", "definition": "Sustainability evaluations ensure governments, corporations, and other stakeholders uphold their commitments, fostering transparency and trust."}, {"term": "Drive innovation", "definition": "Highlighting gaps and challenges through evaluation encourages the creation of new technologies and practices that contribute to a more sustainable future."}, {"term": "Support investment decisions", "definition": "Sustainability metrics support investment decisions, as investors increasingly favour companies with strong sustainability performance as lower-risk, resilient options."}]}, {"number": 5, "text": "A range of criteria for evaluating the sustainability of economic activities", "notes": ["Environmental footprint indicators are metrics used to assess the environmental impact of various activities and processes, providing a comprehensive view of sustainability performance and helping organisations/governments identify areas for improvement.", "Sustainability monitoring can be grouped into social indicators (health & safety, fair wages, gender pay gap, workforce diversity, employee training, risk of corruption, risk of child labor, risk of modern slavery).", "Environmental indicators include greenhouse gas emissions, air pollution, water usage, energy usage, land usage, waste reduction, materials, and biodiversity.", "Economic indicators include GDP contribution, employment, labor productivity, R&D intensity, and R&D value contribution.", "The 17 SDGs can also be used as evaluation criteria, addressing poverty, inequality, climate change, environmental degradation, peace, and justice, providing a shared blueprint for peace and prosperity for people and the planet.", "A 2024 progress assessment found the SDGs are 'severely off track': of 169 targets, 135 can be assessed with available global trend data (2015 baseline to most recent year); 34 lack sufficient trend data."], "caseStudies": [], "data": ["2024 SDG progress assessment: only 17% of assessable targets show progress sufficient for achievement by 2030 ('on track/target met')", "30% of assessable SDG targets show moderate progress; 18% show marginal progress", "48% of assessable SDG targets exhibit moderate-to-severe deviation from the desired trajectory", "18% of assessable SDG targets indicate stagnation; 17% show regression below 2015 baseline levels", "Of 169 SDG targets, 135 can be assessed with available global trend data; 34 lack sufficient trend data"], "flashcards": [{"term": "Environmental footprint indicators", "definition": "Metrics used to assess the environmental impact of activities and processes, giving a comprehensive view of sustainability performance."}, {"term": "Social sustainability indicators", "definition": "Measures such as health & safety, fair wages, gender pay gap, workforce diversity, employee training, and risks of corruption, child labor, or modern slavery."}, {"term": "Environmental sustainability indicators", "definition": "Measures such as greenhouse gas emissions, air pollution, water and energy usage, land usage, waste reduction, materials, and biodiversity."}, {"term": "Economic sustainability indicators", "definition": "Measures such as GDP contribution, employment, labor productivity, and R&D intensity/value contribution."}, {"term": "SDGs as evaluation criteria", "definition": "The 17 SDGs can be used to evaluate sustainability by measuring progress on poverty, inequality, climate change, environmental degradation, peace, and justice; a 2024 assessment found progress 'severely off track', with only 17% of assessable targets on track for 2030."}]}]}, {"sectionTitle": "Investigation of a Global Economic Activity", "dotpoints": [{"number": 6, "text": "The nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity (global tourism)", "notes": ["Tourism is a globalised economic activity worth 7% of total exports worldwide.", "Tourism is big business: international tourism receipts surged from US$2 billion (1950) to US$104 billion (1980) to a pre-pandemic high of US$1.5 trillion (2019); in 2022, receipts were US$1 trillion, making international tourism a major category of international trade.", "Tourism can be international, domestic, seasonal, or diverse in type \u2014 beaches, skiing, eco, wellness, short stay, boutique, sporting, or cultural tourism; 'dark tourism' takes visitors to destinations such as North Korea.", "Tourism creates thousands of direct jobs (retail, food, attractions employees) and indirect jobs (tour organisation, contract laundry, training establishments, planners) worldwide.", "Tourism has global infrastructure and links across primary (resource extraction), secondary (manufactured goods), tertiary (service industry jobs), and quaternary (hi-tech booking and research jobs) economic sectors.", "Spatially, tourism is a global activity: multinational companies such as Disney, Hilton, and Intercontinental hotels operate globally.", "The top 12 tourism earners are MEDCs, with growing influence of India; the USA has the highest international tourism receipts.", "The biggest share of tourists travel to Europe, then Asia/Pacific; Chinese tourists are a fast-growing group; France (particularly Paris) is the top country visited globally."], "caseStudies": [], "data": ["Tourism = 7% of total exports worldwide", "International tourism receipts: US$2 billion (1950) \u2192 US$104 billion (1980) \u2192 US$1.5 trillion (2019, pre-pandemic high) \u2192 US$1 trillion (2022)", "USA had the highest international tourism receipts in 2023: US$189.2 billion"], "flashcards": [{"term": "Dark tourism", "definition": "A form of tourism involving travel to sites associated with death, tragedy, or the macabre \u2014 e.g. destinations like North Korea."}, {"term": "Direct and indirect tourism employment", "definition": "Direct jobs (e.g. retail, food, attractions staff) are created directly by tourist activity, while indirect jobs (e.g. tour organisation, contract laundry, training, planning) support tourism from related industries."}, {"term": "Primary/secondary/tertiary/quaternary tourism-linked jobs", "definition": "Tourism links across economic sectors: primary (resource extraction), secondary (manufactured goods), tertiary (service industry jobs), and quaternary (hi-tech booking and research jobs)."}, {"term": "Tourism receipts growth", "definition": "International tourism receipts grew from US$2 billion in 1950 to a pre-pandemic high of US$1.5 trillion in 2019, reflecting tourism's growth into a major category of international trade."}, {"term": "USA tourism receipts", "definition": "The USA had the world's highest international tourism receipts in 2023, at US$189.2 billion."}]}, {"number": 7, "text": "BETOP influences on the global economic activity", "notes": ["BETOP = Biophysical, Economic, Technological, Organisation(al), and Political factors influencing tourism; each component interacts with the others, creating a complex web of influences on tourism development and management.", "Biophysical factors: climatic conditions, natural resources, and ecological sustainability influence tourism locations and development; tourist behaviour is significantly affected by environmental conditions, impacting destination choice; the Mediterranean climate is often cited as the most favourable for tourism due to its mild weather and seasonal appeal.", "Economic factors: the multiplier effect illustrates how tourism spending stimulates economic activity in other sectors; foreign exchange earnings from international tourism contribute significantly to national economies; advertising promotes destinations via media and cultural narratives; global supply chains provide goods and services for tourism; tourism creates direct and indirect employment, with guest workers filling temporary positions, and tourism growth can drive infrastructure improvements.", "Technological influences: innovations in transportation (mass transport, wide-bodied jets like the Airbus A380, high-speed rail like Japan's Shinkansen) make travel more affordable, accessible and efficient; travel apps help manage bookings and itineraries; social media sharing influences destination popularity; sustainable technologies (renewable energy, electric vehicles) are becoming integral to tourism operations.", "Organisational and political influences: political policy shapes tourism by supporting or restricting industry growth; agencies like Tourism Australia and the UNWTO (UN World Tourism Organization) promote destinations and set standards; visa-free entry agreements simplify travel between countries, enhancing tourism flow."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "BETOP of Kiama, NSW", "facts": ["Biophysical: famous for the Kiama Blowhole and rugged cliffs; temperate climate with mild winters/warm summers; diverse vegetation (rainforests, eucalypt forests, coastal wetlands) supporting flora/fauna, some threatened or endangered.", "Economic: economy driven by tourism, agriculture, and small-scale industries; dairy farming and horticulture are significant; the Kiama Blowhole, beaches and heritage buildings draw visitors year-round.", "Technological: Kiama Council is exploring emerging technologies to improve service delivery, including AI for customer service and planning; benefits from modern transport/communication infrastructure; technology applied to water conservation and waste management.", "Organisational: governed by Kiama Municipal Council, which oversees local planning, development, and community services; committed to sustainable development, community engagement, and managing growth while supporting local businesses.", "Political: part of the electoral district of Kiama in the NSW Legislative Assembly, currently represented by an independent member; local and state government policies significantly impact development, environmental conservation and economic growth."]}], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "BETOP", "definition": "A framework for analysing Biophysical, Economic, Technological, Organisational, and Political influences on tourism development and management."}, {"term": "Multiplier effect", "definition": "The way tourism spending stimulates further economic activity in other sectors of the economy."}, {"term": "Mediterranean climate (tourism)", "definition": "A climate type often cited as the most favourable for tourism, due to its mild weather and seasonal appeal, which attracts visitors and shapes destination choice."}, {"term": "UNWTO", "definition": "The UN World Tourism Organization, an agency that promotes destinations and sets standards for the global tourism industry."}, {"term": "Kiama Blowhole case study", "definition": "Kiama, NSW is a case study of BETOP influences: a biophysically distinctive coastal town (the Kiama Blowhole) whose tourism-driven economy is shaped by council technology adoption, local government organisation, and NSW state/local political influence."}]}, {"number": 8, "text": "Current trends and future directions", "notes": ["Food tourism is a growing trend for tourists to seek unique culinary experiences, connecting with local cultures through food, local ingredients, traditional dishes, cooking classes and food tours; it can drive economic growth in local communities and support farmers and businesses; food festivals and culinary events highlight the importance of gastronomy, and social media plays a significant role in promoting food tourism.", "Wellness tourism is increasing as travellers seek experiences promoting health and well-being, emphasising physical, psychological or spiritual activities such as yoga retreats and spa experiences; demand is driven by growing awareness of health issues and self-care, with destinations offering wellness-focused packages.", "The outbound market is a significant future trend: the size and increasing wealth of the middle class in developing countries is reshaping the tourism industry, with emerging markets becoming significant sources of outbound tourists and technology transforming travel planning and booking.", "Solo travel is an increasing trend driven by personal growth, adventure and relaxation; solo travellers seek unique experiences and self-discovery; social media has created supportive communities for solo travellers; safety and accessibility are key considerations, and destinations catering to solo travellers can enhance their appeal.", "The rise of the LGBTQIA+ community as a travel market reflects broader societal changes; destinations offering safe, welcoming environments are likely to see increased LGBTQIA+ tourism; representation and visibility in marketing is important; events such as Pride festivals can significantly boost tourism in host cities, and LGBTQIA+ travel agencies/tour operators are rising to meet demand."], "caseStudies": [], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Food tourism", "definition": "A growing trend where tourists seek unique culinary experiences to connect with local cultures through food, cooking classes, and food tours, boosting local economies."}, {"term": "Wellness tourism", "definition": "A growing trend where travellers seek experiences promoting physical, psychological or spiritual health and well-being, such as yoga retreats and spa experiences."}, {"term": "Outbound market / emerging markets", "definition": "The growing size and wealth of the middle class in developing countries is producing significant new sources of outbound tourists, reshaping global tourism patterns."}, {"term": "Solo travel", "definition": "An increasing trend of travelling alone, driven by personal growth, adventure and self-discovery, supported by online communities and requiring attention to safety and accessibility."}, {"term": "LGBTQIA+ tourism", "definition": "A growing travel market seeking inclusive, safe and welcoming destinations, with representation in marketing and events like Pride festivals boosting tourism in host cities."}]}]}, {"sectionTitle": "Sustainability of a Global Economic Activity", "dotpoints": [{"number": 9, "text": "Evaluate the sustainability of the activity, using one or more criteria", "notes": ["Sustainable tourism encompasses travel practices that consider economic, social, and environmental impacts, aiming to minimise negative effects while maximising benefits for local communities; it promotes responsible travel that conserves natural resources, respects local cultures, and supports local economies.", "The concept of sustainable tourism emerged as a response to the adverse effects of mass tourism, which often leads to environmental degradation and cultural erosion.", "Mass tourism refers to high volumes of tourists visiting popular destinations, leading to significant environmental and cultural changes.", "Eco-tourism is a form of sustainable tourism emphasising responsible travel to natural areas, conserving the environment, and improving local well-being.", "Greenwashing is the practice of companies making misleading claims about their environmental practices to appear more sustainable than they are, misleading the public and undermining genuine environmental initiatives.", "Economic unsustainability \u2014 tourism leakage: holiday spending passes through several stages (total holiday expenses, in-country expenses, local destination expenses), with money leaking out at each stage via online travel agencies, hotel chains, destination management companies, and imported goods, rather than benefiting the local destination.", "Cultural/social unsustainability \u2014 Doxey's Index of Irritation (Irridex) models stages of host-community attitudes as tourism develops: Euphoria (visitors and investors welcomed, little control), Apathy (visitors taken for granted, relations become commercial), Annoyance (saturation approached, residents have misgivings, policymakers add infrastructure rather than limiting growth), and Antagonism (irritations openly expressed, visitors blamed for problems, remedial planning alongside increased promotion to offset reputational damage).", "Cultural commodification is the process where local traditions, customs and cultural expressions are packaged and commercialised for tourists, often losing authenticity \u2014 leading to staged performances, souvenir production and altered rituals that prioritise profit over cultural integrity.", "Environmental impacts of tourism are both negative (habitat destruction, deforestation, biodiversity loss from infrastructure development; increased waste, pollution and carbon emissions; overcrowding causing soil erosion and wildlife disturbance; overexploitation of water resources) and positive (funding conservation projects, promoting environmental awareness, supporting protection of natural parks and heritage sites) when tourism is managed sustainably."], "caseStudies": [], "data": ["In developing countries it is not uncommon for over 90% of tourism dollars spent by vacationing tourists to leak out of the destination, leaving less than 10% to benefit locally"], "flashcards": [{"term": "Mass tourism", "definition": "High volumes of tourists visiting popular destinations, leading to significant environmental and cultural changes."}, {"term": "Eco-tourism", "definition": "A form of sustainable tourism emphasising responsible travel to natural areas, conserving the environment, and improving local well-being."}, {"term": "Greenwashing", "definition": "The practice of companies making misleading claims about their environmental practices to appear more sustainable than they really are."}, {"term": "Tourism leakage", "definition": "The process by which tourist spending exits the destination economy through foreign-owned agencies, hotel chains and imported goods; in developing countries over 90% of tourism dollars can leak out, leaving under 10% to benefit locals."}, {"term": "Doxey's Irridex", "definition": "A model of host-community attitudes to tourism development, moving through four stages: Euphoria, Apathy, Annoyance, and Antagonism."}, {"term": "Cultural commodification", "definition": "The packaging and commercialisation of local traditions and cultural expressions for tourists, often losing authenticity through staged performances and altered rituals."}]}, {"number": 10, "text": "Examine a range of strategies for sustainability in the activity", "notes": ["Fly less, stay longer: reduces carbon emissions from frequent flights, encourages deeper engagement with local culture and environment, and supports local economies through extended stays.", "Slow tourism: focuses on quality experiences over quantity of destinations, promotes walking, cycling and local transport instead of high-impact travel, and reduces environmental footprint and stress on ecosystems.", "Eco-tourism: prioritises conservation and community benefits, often involves nature-based activities with minimal environmental impact, and educates travellers about sustainability and local ecosystems.", "Quotas: limit the number of visitors to sensitive areas to prevent over-tourism, protecting fragile ecosystems and cultural heritage sites while helping maintain quality of visitor experience.", "National Parks and conservation areas: designated zones for protecting biodiversity and landscapes, providing controlled access and guidelines for sustainable tourism, and generating revenue for conservation through entrance fees and permits.", "Organisational Sustainable Development Guidelines: frameworks for balancing tourism's economic, social and environmental aspects; Tourism Evaluation Criteria are standards for assessing the sustainability of tourism activities against environmental and social benchmarks."], "caseStudies": [], "data": [], "flashcards": [{"term": "Fly less, stay longer", "definition": "A sustainability strategy that reduces carbon emissions from frequent flights by encouraging longer stays, deeper cultural engagement, and greater local economic support."}, {"term": "Slow tourism", "definition": "A strategy focusing on quality experiences over quantity of destinations, promoting walking, cycling and local transport to reduce environmental footprint."}, {"term": "Quotas (tourism strategy)", "definition": "Limits placed on visitor numbers to sensitive areas to prevent over-tourism, protecting ecosystems and heritage sites while maintaining visitor experience quality."}, {"term": "National parks and conservation areas", "definition": "Designated zones that protect biodiversity and landscapes while providing controlled, sustainable tourist access and generating conservation revenue through fees and permits."}, {"term": "Tourism Evaluation Criteria", "definition": "Standards used within organisational sustainable development guidelines to assess whether tourism activities meet environmental and social benchmarks."}]}, {"number": 11, "text": "Critically analyse ONE strategy", "notes": ["Thredbo Alpine Resort (NSW) is the guide's fully worked case study strategy, evaluated across the environmental, social, economic and cultural pillars of sustainability.", "Model judgment paragraph (from the guide): Thredbo demonstrates a high level of sustainability across environmental, social, economic and cultural dimensions, making it a leading example of sustainable tourism in Australia, balancing environmental protection with social and economic benefits while respecting cultural heritage.", "'Evaluate [X] using clear criteria and add a judgement' style questions require stating criteria explicitly and ending with a clear judgement/conclusion, as modelled by the Thredbo paragraph."], "caseStudies": [{"name": "Thredbo Alpine Resort (NSW)", "facts": ["Environmental: operates on 100% renewable energy and uses biodegradable packaging to replace single-use plastics; implements closed-loop organic recycling, reducing waste and producing compost; uses SNOWsat technology for efficient snow grooming, saving energy and water.", "Environmental: partners with Greenfleet, offsetting over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting approximately 45,000 native trees; employs electric vehicles in resort operations; holds Gold EarthCheck certification and restores alpine habitats within Kosciuszko National Park.", "Social: runs Environment Week and community engagement programs to raise sustainability awareness; provides year-round employment, reducing seasonal job insecurity; promotes health and well-being through outdoor activities and wellness programs; collaborates with local communities and stakeholders for inclusive decision-making.", "Economic: diversifies income beyond winter skiing with summer activities like mountain biking, hiking, and fishing; hosts over 100 annual events, boosting tourism and supporting local businesses; positions itself as a 'green destination', attracting eco-conscious travellers.", "Cultural: recognises and respects the Monero\u2013Ngarigo people, traditional custodians of the land; incorporates Indigenous heritage into visitor education and sustainability programs; promotes cultural awareness through interpretation and signage."]}], "data": ["Thredbo's Greenfleet partnership has offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting approximately 45,000 native trees", "Thredbo hosts over 100 events annually", "Thredbo holds Gold EarthCheck certification"], "flashcards": [{"term": "Thredbo Alpine Resort", "definition": "A NSW ski resort used as a case study strategy for sustainable tourism, demonstrating strong environmental, social, economic and cultural sustainability practices."}, {"term": "Greenfleet (Thredbo partnership)", "definition": "An organisation Thredbo partners with to offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting around 45,000 native trees."}, {"term": "Gold EarthCheck certification", "definition": "A sustainability certification held by Thredbo Alpine Resort, reflecting its high standard of environmental and sustainability management."}, {"term": "SNOWsat technology", "definition": "Technology used by Thredbo for efficient snow grooming, saving energy and water."}, {"term": "Monero\u2013Ngarigo people", "definition": "The traditional custodians of the land on which Thredbo Alpine Resort operates, recognised and respected through the resort's cultural sustainability initiatives."}]}]}]};
window.topic1WrittenData = [{"id": "hsc2025_q20a", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [3], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(a)):\n3 marks - Describes the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability\n2 marks - Outlines the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global forums help international collaboration by creating time and space to discuss issues and set shared sustainability goals. They provide a platform for countries to align policies, share best practices, and commit to environmental targets to address issues like climate change and poverty reduction."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q20b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 5, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Answer parts (b) and (c) with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Discuss the effect of ONE influence on the global economic activity identified.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(b)):\n5 marks - Discusses the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n4 marks - Explains the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n3 marks - Outlines the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n2 marks - Describes an influence on a global economic activity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer:\nActivity: Mining\nTechnology advances significantly influence global mining operations. Innovations in automation, remote sensing and AI can improve efficiency of production and resource use resulting in higher profits and growth in the activity. However, these innovations have high initial costs and investment which favour larger mining companies in less regulated countries."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q20c", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 5, "directive": "Compare", "question": "Compare TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in the global economic activity identified.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(c)):\n5 marks - Provides a thorough comparison of TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n4 marks - Provides some comparison of TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n3 marks - Outlines TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n2 marks - Identifies TWO strategies or outlines ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One strategy for sustainability in the mining industry is utilising more efficient technologies to recycle materials, such as metals and minerals, to reduce the need for new extraction and therefore reduce resource use. This helps conserve natural resources and lowers environmental impact. Another strategy is reducing energy consumption by using renewable energy sources like solar or wind power in mining operations. Both approaches aim to minimise environmental harm, but recycling focuses on resource conservation, while energy reduction targets lowering emissions and energy costs."}, {"id": "sample_17b", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [3], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Many medicines have been developed using Indigenous Peoples' knowledges of the medicinal properties of plants.\n\nExplain how benefit sharing presents opportunities for the sustainable production of plant-based medicines.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 4 marks): Demonstrates a thorough understanding of how benefit sharing presents opportunities for the sustainable production of plant-based medicines. (3 marks: sound understanding; 2 marks: basic understanding; 1 mark: some relevant information.) Official sample answer: Benefit sharing agreements provide for the ongoing and equitable distribution of benefits arising from the sharing of knowledges, practices and resources. Indigenous Peoples' sharing of knowledges and practices with research institutions or medical companies could result in advancements in treatments and an increased awareness of sustainable production methods to ensure ongoing availability of raw materials. Research institutions could learn from Indigenous Peoples' methods of cultivating plants and transferring raw materials into treatments, and medical companies could share production technologies to enhance efficiency. Economic benefits of these agreements could include supply contracts and corporate partnerships."}, {"id": "sample_20b", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain potential impacts of the global fast fashion industry on either aquatic or land-based ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 5 marks): Demonstrates a thorough understanding of how and why the fast fashion industry could impact ecosystems; relates impacts to aquatic OR land-based ecosystems in detail. (Lower bands, 4-1 marks, require progressively more basic/limited understanding.) Official sample answer (land-based ecosystems): The high volume of fast fashion waste increases demand for landfill sites and creates pressure to clear land to produce more fibre to satisfy increasing demand for fashion. These activities contribute to the loss of terrestrial ecosystems such as forests, grasslands and freshwater wetlands, reducing their spatial extent and increasing their vulnerability to further change. Ecosystems fragmented by land clearing, such as eucalypt forests, are more vulnerable to the edge effects of settlement and agriculture such as exotic species and feral animals. Waste in landfill releases methane, a greenhouse gas, which, along with the CO2 produced by production and transportation, affects ecosystem functioning by altering the physical conditions in which an ecosystem can survive."}, {"id": "sample_20c", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 6, "directive": "With reference to a global economic activity you have studied, analyse", "question": "With reference to a global economic activity you have studied, analyse ONE strategy used to achieve social or environmental sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 6 marks): Makes clear reference to a global economic activity; clearly draws out and relates implications of ONE strategy used to achieve social or environmental sustainability. (Lower bands, 5-1 marks, require progressively less developed reference/analysis, down to identifying/describing only.) Official sample answer: Cruise ship tourism threatens the environmental sustainability of marine ecosystems through its impact on biodiversity. In Glacier Bay National Park in Alaska, USA tourists come to see wildlife whose survival depends on a healthy marine environment. Pollution from wastewater and ballast discharge, fuel leakage and biocides from ships, as well as the noise, lights, and physical disturbance of wildlife and habitats by tourists needed effective management. The US government agency managing cruise shipping introduced limited entry permits to reduce environmental impacts and increase compliance with environmental regulations such as waste disposal. Every ten years, cruise operators must submit a proposal to visit. The strategy has been effective because new contracts are awarded to operators with a record of following environmental regulations. As a result of intense competition for limited permits, cruise tourism in Alaska has become more environmentally sustainable."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q17a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer parts (a) and (b) with reference to ONE global economic activity you have studied. Global economic activity: ______\n(a) Outline ONE influence on the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (2 marks): Correctly identifies a global economic activity and accurately outlines ONE influence on this activity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information related to influences on the global economic activity - 1 mark. Other influences could include: Biophysical; Economic; Technological; Political/organisational. Sample Answer (Global Economic Activity: Tourism): Technology plays a significant role in influencing the global economic activity of tourism. Information technology has transformed how tourists plan, book, and experience their trips. Online booking platforms like Expedia and Booking.com simplify the reservation process, while mobile apps such as Google Maps and TripAdvisor guide tourists in planning itineraries, navigating destinations, and finding top attractions or dining options."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q17b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Answer parts (a) and (b) with reference to ONE global economic activity you have studied. Global economic activity: ______\n(b) Describe ONE current trend of the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (3 marks): Provides a detailed description of one current trend on the global economic activity - 3 marks; Provides some characteristics and features of a current trend in the global economic activity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information related to a trend - 1 mark. Sample Answer: A current trend in global tourism is wellness travel. According to National Geographic, 21% of travelers in 2022 were motivated by health or wellness-related reasons. This trend is expected to continue, with Expedia predicting a 30% increase in demand, particularly for wellness retreats. These retreats cater to a wide range of needs. For example, Foma in Byron Bay offers yoga, meditation, and ayurvedic treatments with plant-based meals. In contrast, the Sofitel Hotel in Darling Harbour, Sydney, provides a half-day wellness journey, combining exercise classes with pampering treatments, such as a Biologique Recherche facial or infrared sauna. Wellness travel is an emerging trend in the global tourism industry."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [2], "marks": 2, "directive": "Define", "question": "Define the precautionary principle, providing an example.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Accurately defines, using an example, the precautionary principle (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: The precautionary principle involves taking early action to prevent environmental harm, even if scientific evidence is not fully certain. In a kelp forest ecosystem like the Great Southern Reef, authorities might limit coastal development or ban certain fishing practices if there's a risk they could damage kelp beds, even before conclusive evidence proves the harm."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18b", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [5, 9], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of ONE global economic activity to answer Questions 18(b)-(c). Explain how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Clearly explains how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity; shows a clear understanding of global sustainability (4 marks); Describes how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity (3 marks); Outlines criteria that can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: One form of criteria to evaluate the sustainability of cotton farming are the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG's). For example, SDG 12 'responsible consumption and production' could be used to determine the economic and environmental sustainability of cotton farming as it could measure the amount of waste in the primary phase of production. It could also be used to determine the amount of cotton that goes to landfill when producing textiles, which could result in the industry implementing a circular economy to minimise its ecological footprint and improve its reputation. Others could include: Carbon footprint; Water footprint; Pillars of sustainability (SEEC); Principles of ecologically sustainable development (ESD); Certification; Circular economy."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18c", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 5, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the nature and spatial patterns of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Clearly provides reasons for the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity; demonstrates deep knowledge and understanding of an economic activity (5 marks); Attempts to provide reasons for the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity; demonstrates knowledge and understanding of an economic activity (4 marks); Describes the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity (3 marks); Outlines the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: Viticulture is an agricultural activity involving the cultivation of Vitis vinifera for the production and consumption of wine. It is a highly skilled and mechanised industry, influenced by globalisation and technological innovation in farming. This has led to viticulture now covering over 7.4 million hectares of vineyards globally, with more than 45% of wine bottles crossing international borders to both established (Japan) and new markets (India and China). Viticulture has historically been an Old World phenomenon, with Spain, France and Italy dominating global production, as wine is inherently connected to cultural norms such as the Mediterranean diet. However, the industry has expanded into New World markets, including Australia, New Zealand, Chile, Argentina and China. Today, wine is produced in over 90 countries, generating approximately 272 million hectolitres of wine annually (OIV, 2024). Wine is typically grown in the 'wine belt' between 40-50N and 30-40S of the equator in Mediterranean climates characterised by long, warm to hot summers and cool, wet winters. These conditions support optimal sugar production, as the enzymes responsible function best between 18-33C, resulting in complex and character-rich wines. This has led to the emergence of globally iconic regions, such as Shiraz from the Barossa Valley (Australia) and Riesling from Mosel (Germany)."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_01", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline the nature of global tourism as a global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Outlines the nature of global tourism as a global economic activity\n2 marks - Describes an aspect of the nature of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global tourism is a global economic activity involving the movement of people to destinations outside their usual environment for leisure, business or other purposes. It generates income and employment through accommodation, transport, food and recreation services, and ranges in scale from small heritage towns like Kiama, NSW, to large international resort destinations, making it one of the world's largest and most geographically widespread service industries."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_02", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns of global tourism.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Describes the spatial patterns of global tourism with named examples\n3 marks - Describes the spatial patterns of global tourism\n2 marks - Outlines a spatial pattern of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global tourism flows are highly uneven, concentrated around coastal and natural-attraction destinations, historic/cultural cities, and alpine or resort regions. Flows are strongest from wealthy, developed source countries towards both nearby and distant destinations, often clustering around transport hubs (airports, highways). In NSW, this pattern is visible at a smaller scale: coastal towns such as Kiama attract day-trippers and short-stay visitors from Sydney and Wollongong due to their proximity and coastal attractions (the Blowhole, beaches), while alpine destinations such as Thredbo attract longer-stay, seasonal visitor flows tied to the snow season and, increasingly, summer activities."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_03", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline how technological factors have influenced global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Outlines how technological factors have influenced global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n2 marks - Describes a technological factor affecting global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Technological factors have influenced tourism at Kiama through Kiama Council's exploration of emerging technologies such as AI for customer service and planning, alongside modern transport and communication infrastructure that make the town easier for visitors to reach and navigate. Technology is also applied to water conservation and waste management, helping the town manage the environmental pressure that a high volume of day-trip and short-stay tourists places on local infrastructure."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_04", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how political and organisational factors have affected the spatial patterns of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5 marks - Explains how political and organisational factors have affected the spatial patterns of global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n4 marks - Explains ONE of political or organisational factors, with some reference to the other\n3 marks - Describes political and/or organisational factors affecting tourism\n2 marks - Outlines a factor affecting tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Kiama is governed by Kiama Municipal Council, an organisational factor that directly shapes where and how tourism development occurs through local planning and development approvals, and through its stated commitment to sustainable development and managing growth while supporting local businesses. Politically, Kiama sits within the NSW Legislative Assembly electoral district of Kiama, currently held by an independent member; local and state government policies on development and environmental conservation significantly influence which areas are protected from development (concentrating tourism around existing attractions like the Blowhole and heritage Queen Street-style precincts) and which are opened up for new visitor infrastructure, directly shaping the spatial pattern of where tourism activity is permitted to grow."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_05", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how biophysical and economic factors have influenced the nature of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains how biophysical and economic factors have influenced the nature of global tourism, with well-integrated reference to Kiama\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE of biophysical or economic factors, or describes both\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Biophysical factors are central to Kiama's tourism identity: the famous Kiama Blowhole and rugged coastal cliffs are the town's signature natural attraction, while its temperate climate (mild winters, warm summers) and diverse vegetation, including rainforest, eucalypt forest and coastal wetlands, support both scenic appeal and nature-based activities. Some of this vegetation supports threatened or endangered species, which shapes the nature of tourism towards low-impact, nature-viewing activity rather than intensive development. Economically, tourism operates alongside agriculture (dairy farming) and horticulture as core industries, and the Blowhole, beaches and heritage buildings draw visitors year-round, supporting a service economy of cafes, accommodation and retail. Together, these factors have produced a tourism industry centred on short, nature- and heritage-based visits rather than large-scale resort tourism."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_06", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 20, "directive": "Examine", "question": "Examine the changing nature, spatial patterns and future directions of global tourism, with reference to examples you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n17-20 marks - Provides a comprehensive and well-supported examination of the changing nature, spatial patterns and future directions of global tourism, integrating specific, accurate case study evidence throughout\n13-16 marks - Provides a well-supported examination addressing most of nature, spatial patterns and future directions, with generally accurate case study evidence\n9-12 marks - Provides some explanation of aspects of global tourism's nature, spatial patterns and/or future directions, with limited case study evidence\n5-8 marks - Describes global tourism with limited reference to nature, spatial patterns or future directions\n1-4 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer (approx. 380 words): Global tourism has changed substantially in nature over recent decades, shifting from mass, package-style holidays towards more individualised, experience-based and sustainability-conscious travel. This is visible at Kiama, NSW, where tourism has evolved from simple day-trip sightseeing around the Blowhole towards a broader visitor economy built on heritage precincts, food and nature-based experiences, supported by Kiama Council's growing use of technology (including exploring AI for customer service and planning) and continued investment in water conservation and waste management to handle rising visitor numbers sustainably.\n\nSpatial patterns in global tourism remain strongly uneven, concentrated around coastal, natural and alpine attractions and major transport corridors. At a national scale, Kiama's coastal location within easy reach of Sydney and Wollongong produces a pattern of short-stay, high-frequency visitation, while Thredbo Alpine Resort's more remote alpine location in Kosciuszko National Park produces a smaller number of longer, seasonally concentrated stays tied to the snow season. Thredbo's response of diversifying into summer activities such as mountain biking, hiking and fishing, and hosting over 100 annual events, illustrates a broader global trend of resorts extending their tourist season and spatial drawing area beyond a single peak period.\n\nFuture directions for both destinations point towards deeper integration of sustainability into tourism's core offering rather than treating it as an add-on. Thredbo's Gold EarthCheck certification, 100% renewable energy operation, closed-loop organic recycling, and partnership with Greenfleet (offsetting over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions through planting roughly 45,000 native trees) all position it as a 'green destination' aimed at attracting increasingly eco-conscious travellers - a market segment expected to keep growing. Kiama's future direction is likely to focus on managing growth so that increasing visitor numbers do not compromise the natural attractions (threatened/endangered species habitat, coastal ecosystems) that tourism itself depends on, consistent with Kiama Council's stated commitment to sustainable development. Together, these examples suggest global tourism's future lies less in maximising visitor volume and more in maximising the quality, sustainability and year-round spread of visitation."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_07", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe ONE environmental impact of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Describes an environmental impact of global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n2 marks - Outlines an environmental impact of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: A key environmental impact of tourism at Kiama is pressure on sensitive coastal ecosystems, including rainforest, eucalypt forest and coastal wetland vegetation that supports some threatened or endangered species. Rising visitor numbers around attractions like the Blowhole increase foot traffic, litter and demand on water and waste infrastructure, which is why Kiama Council has had to invest in technology for water conservation and waste management to reduce tourism's footprint on these environments."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_08", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the social impact and economic impact of global tourism, with reference to a place you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains both a social impact and an economic impact of global tourism, with well-integrated reference to a place studied\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE impact, or describes both\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: At Kiama, tourism has a significant economic impact: alongside dairy farming and horticulture, it is a core driver of the local economy, supporting cafes, accommodation, retail and heritage-building based businesses that rely on year-round visitor spending, and Kiama Council actively invests in infrastructure to sustain this. Socially, tourism brings both benefits and costs to the community: it supports local employment and community identity around the town's heritage and natural attractions, but high visitor volumes, especially on weekends, can strain local infrastructure and create congestion that affects residents' quality of life, which is part of why the council frames its approach around 'managing growth while supporting local businesses' rather than simply maximising visitor numbers."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_09", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe TWO strategies used to improve the sustainability of global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Describes TWO strategies used to improve the sustainability of tourism at Thredbo\n3 marks - Describes ONE strategy, or outlines TWO\n2 marks - Outlines ONE strategy\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One strategy at Thredbo is operating on 100% renewable energy and using biodegradable packaging to replace single-use plastics, directly reducing the resort's carbon footprint and waste stream. A second strategy is diversifying income beyond winter skiing into summer activities such as mountain biking, hiking and fishing, which spreads visitor demand (and therefore environmental and infrastructure pressure) more evenly across the year rather than concentrating it in a short peak season, while also making the resort less economically vulnerable to a poor snow season."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_10", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain TWO factors that have contributed to the success of a sustainability strategy in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains TWO factors contributing to the success of a named sustainability strategy at Thredbo\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE factor, or describes TWO\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Thredbo's environmental sustainability strategy has been supported by strong organisational commitment, shown through achieving Gold EarthCheck certification and partnering with Greenfleet to offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting around 45,000 native trees - external accreditation and partnership that both validate the strategy and create ongoing accountability. A second contributing factor is technological investment, such as SNOWsat technology for more efficient snow grooming, which saves energy and water while maintaining the ski product visitors expect; because this technology delivers a cost saving alongside an environmental one, it gives the resort a strong ongoing incentive to maintain and extend the strategy rather than treating it as a one-off initiative."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_11", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 4, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Outlines a named strategy for achieving sustainability in tourism, with reference to Thredbo\n3 marks - Describes an aspect of the strategy\n2 marks - Identifies the strategy\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Thredbo Alpine Resort's environmental sustainability strategy centres on decarbonising its operations: it runs on 100% renewable energy, has partnered with Greenfleet to offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions through planting approximately 45,000 native trees, employs electric vehicles in resort operations, and holds Gold EarthCheck certification. The strategy also extends to habitat restoration within Kosciuszko National Park and closed-loop organic recycling that turns waste into compost, reflecting a broad approach that targets emissions, waste and biodiversity together rather than any single issue in isolation."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_12", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 20, "directive": "Critically analyse", "question": "Critically analyse ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n17-20 marks - Provides a sophisticated, well-substantiated critical analysis of the strategy, weighing its strengths against limitations and using precise case study evidence throughout\n13-16 marks - Provides a well-supported analysis of the strategy with some critical judgement and generally accurate evidence\n9-12 marks - Describes the strategy with limited critical judgement, some case study evidence\n5-8 marks - Describes the strategy with limited case study evidence\n1-4 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer (approx. 360 words): Thredbo Alpine Resort's environmental sustainability strategy is a genuinely multi-pronged response to the ecological risks alpine tourism creates, and it succeeds on several measures. Environmentally, the resort operates on 100% renewable energy, uses SNOWsat technology to make snow grooming more energy- and water-efficient, and runs closed-loop organic recycling that turns waste into compost rather than sending it to landfill. Its partnership with Greenfleet, which has offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting roughly 45,000 native trees, together with Gold EarthCheck certification, gives the strategy external verification rather than relying on the resort's own claims - a genuine strength, since greenwashing is a common criticism of tourism sustainability strategies elsewhere. The strategy also restores alpine habitat within Kosciuszko National Park, directly addressing biodiversity loss rather than only carbon emissions.\n\nEconomically and socially, the strategy is reinforced by diversifying income beyond winter skiing into mountain biking, hiking and fishing, and hosting over 100 annual events; this both reduces the resort's vulnerability to poor snow seasons (a growing risk under climate change) and provides more consistent, year-round local employment, addressing the seasonal job insecurity common in alpine tourism. Positioning as a 'green destination' also appears to be commercially effective, attracting eco-conscious travellers as a distinct market segment.\n\nHowever, the strategy has limitations worth weighing critically. Alpine tourism is inherently reliant on a shrinking snow season under climate change, meaning even a highly sustainable resort operates within an activity whose long-term physical basis is under threat - sustainability initiatives address the resort's own footprint but cannot resolve this deeper structural vulnerability. There is also a question of scale: tree planting and habitat restoration, while valuable, are modest compared to the scale of emissions from visitor transport to and from the resort, which the strategy does not appear to directly address. Overall, Thredbo's strategy is a strong, well-evidenced example of tourism sustainability management, but its success should be understood as significantly reducing the resort's own operational impact rather than resolving the sector's broader sustainability challenges."}, {"id": "trial2026-w10", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Outline ONE future trend in the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE future trend in a global economic activity. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information about a global economic activity.\n\nSample answer: One future trend in tourism is growth in the outbound market from developing countries, as rising incomes and an expanding middle class \u2014 particularly in Asia \u2014 reshape global travel patterns, with more residents of these nations becoming international tourists rather than only receiving them."}, {"id": "trial2026-w11", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9, 10], "marks": 6, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Answer with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Discuss TWO strategies businesses or governments can use to manage the environmental sustainability of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Clearly discusses TWO strategies businesses or governments can use for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 5 marks \u2013 Discusses TWO strategies businesses or governments can use for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 4 marks \u2013 Describes TWO strategies for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 2\u20133 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE or more strategies for sustainability within the global economic activity. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information to question.\n\nSample answer: Two strategies used to improve the environmental sustainability of global tourism are visitor quotas and renewable energy transition, both demonstrated by Thredbo Alpine Resort. Visitor quotas are effective because they limit tourist numbers in environmentally sensitive areas, reducing erosion, habitat damage and waste generation. However, critics argue that quotas can restrict visitor access and reduce income for tourism businesses that depend on high visitor numbers. Renewable energy transition involves tourism operators using cleaner energy sources to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Thredbo operates on 100% renewable energy and uses technologies that reduce water and energy consumption. This strategy is highly effective in lowering the industry's environmental impact; however, the high installation costs can make adoption difficult for smaller operators. Overall, both strategies make a significant contribution to sustainable tourism."}];
window.topic1CaseStudiesData = [{"name": "Corporate sustainability challenges \u2014 Nestl\u00e9, Microsoft, Amazon", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [3], "facts": ["Nestl\u00e9 (Consumer Goods): faces criticism over single-use plastics and water extraction practices, especially in vulnerable regions.", "Microsoft (Tech): data centres consume vast amounts of energy; while the company is shifting to renewables, hardware production still generates significant e-waste.", "Amazon (Retail): massive packaging volumes and carbon emissions from logistics pose sustainability challenges despite the company's renewable energy investments.", "Presented in the deck as a 'challenges' counterpart to the corporate 'opportunities' examples (Air New Zealand, Tesla, IKEA), illustrating that different industry sectors face distinct sustainability challenges (plastic/water, e-waste/energy, packaging/logistics)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "UN Global Compact", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [3], "facts": ["Described as the world's largest corporate sustainability initiative.", "Encourages businesses worldwide to align their operations with ten universal principles covering human rights, labour, environment, and anti-corruption.", "By joining, companies commit to responsible practices and contribute to broader UN goals, including the SDGs.", "Promotes collaboration between businesses, governments and civil society to create a more sustainable and inclusive global economy.", "Presented as a 'major opportunity' for corporate action on sustainability, distinct from individual company case studies."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "IKEA \u2014 circular economy model", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [3], "facts": ["Runs an 'As-Is' centre selling returned, recycled, 'seconds' furniture and ex-display models.", "'BuyBack 365' scheme: customers can sell back used IKEA furniture year-round.", "'Care and repair' service provides tools to fix furniture and supplies spare parts, offering practical help to extend product life ('second life' / proactive slogan; chipboard products priced appropriately).", "Uses recycling and renewable energy in its operations.", "Aims to become climate positive by 2030; offers furniture take-back/recycling programs; invests in renewable energy and sustainable forestry.", "The deck explicitly poses 'the irony of IKEA' as a discussion point \u2014 students are asked to identify IKEA's actions for sustainability, weigh opportunities/challenges, and make an evaluative comment (i.e. a fast-fashion-style furniture retailer marketing itself as circular/sustainable)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Global salmon aquaculture industry", "category": "Concept/Term", "relatedDotPoints": [4, 5], "facts": ["Used as the deck's fully worked example for 'evaluating sustainability', nicknamed 'the battery-hen farming of the sea'.", "80% of salmon consumed today are farmed; production process: eggs fertilised and incubated on land, raised in freshwater hatcheries for 6\u201312 months, undergo 'smoltification', then transferred to open sea net pens (thousands of fish each) for 12\u201318 months before harvest, transport and processing.", "Traditionally farmed in cold-water countries (8\u201314\u00b0C) \u2014 Norway, Canada, Chile, Sweden; today also farmed at scale in Australia, the US and China.", "The Aquaculture Stewardship Council (ASC) is the largest certification body for salmon and uses over 154 criteria to evaluate farms (e.g. local biodiversity under SDG14, carbon footprint under SDG12).", "90 million metric tonnes of farmed salmon were produced in 2023, helping meet growing food security demand for protein, though farmed salmon has higher fat content than wild-caught.", "Provides over 21 million employment opportunities globally; new farms can improve rural infrastructure and reduce poverty in rural communities (per cited research)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Salmon farming \u2014 environmental impacts (escapees, disease, dead zones)", "category": "Place", "relatedDotPoints": [4, 5, 2], "facts": ["Positive: farming reduces pressure on wild fish populations compared to wild capture.", "Negative: over 3.3 million farmed salmon have escaped since 2010; escapees interbreed with wild fish (causing genetic mutation), and spread disease and parasites/pests such as sea lice.", "Research cited shows wild salmon population survival rates drop by up to 50% in regions near farmed salmon operations.", "Excessive fecal matter from net pens can accumulate, increasing organic decomposition and reducing dissolved oxygen, creating 'dead zones' and possible algal blooms \u2014 example given: Tasmania's Macquarie Harbour.", "Used in the deck as evidence to weigh against the local biodiversity (SDG14) evaluation criterion, feeding into a 'make a judgement' step on environmental sustainability."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Salmon farming \u2014 carbon footprint comparison", "category": "Data/Statistic", "relatedDotPoints": [5], "facts": ["Positive: salmon farming disturbs 9x less area (103,500 km\u00b2) while yielding 55 times greater production than comparable land-based farming.", "New technologies (e.g. supercooling) are reducing refrigeration/transport emissions, cutting related GHG output by 17%.", "Converting fish meal into protein is 5x more efficient than producing beef protein.", "Negative: traditional transportation emits significant GHGs via cargo ships, planes and refrigeration; supply chain inefficiencies exist \u2014 e.g. Alaskan-caught fish is sent to China for processing before being distributed globally.", "Land-based salmon farms require extensive electricity to run filtration systems."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Salmon farming \u2014 cultural and economic impacts (Kaw\u00e9sqar Reserve, Chile & British Columbia, Canada)", "category": "Place", "relatedDotPoints": [3, 4, 5], "facts": ["Kaw\u00e9sqar National Reserve, southern Chile: salmon farm operators claim to occupy only 0.06% of the Indigenous reserve, but First Nation Kaw\u00e9sqar communities accuse the farms of violating land rights and polluting sacred sites.", "British Columbia, Canada is implementing agreements with First Nations groups over salmon farming; First Nations now approve 75% of fish farmed in the province.", "Presented as the 'CULTURAL: Cultural Heritage' criterion in a holistic (multi-pillar) evaluation of salmon aquaculture's sustainability, alongside economic (employment) and social (food security) criteria.", "Illustrates a direct tension between Indigenous land rights/benefit sharing and the expansion of a global economic activity \u2014 relevant to both the tourism/economic-activity dot points and the Indigenous practices/benefit-sharing dot point."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Recirculating Aquaculture Systems (RAS)", "category": "Concept/Term", "relatedDotPoints": [5, 10], "facts": ["Close-containment tanks that allow salmon to be farmed entirely on land, avoiding ocean net pens.", "Growing rapidly because farmers can manage environmental constraints (climate, pests, disease, escapees) more tightly; used mainly in the United States, Japan, China and the United Arab Emirates.", "Approximately 500,000 tonnes of salmon projected to be farmed via RAS by 2050.", "Environmental benefits: reduces cross-breeding/disease/pest spread (SDG14); residual waste can be converted to energy or fertiliser; lower transport emissions (closer to markets); continual water treatment/filtering/reuse reduces ecological footprint (SDG12). Environmental costs: requires extensive electricity, infrastructure, land and materials (pumps, filters, biofilters, UV/ozone injectors) and creates waste-disposal challenges (diseased water, saltwater discharge, sludge).", "Economic (SDG8): creates new rural jobs and infrastructure, but requires highly trained workers (disadvantaging smaller/untrained farmers, particularly in Chile) and high setup costs risk creating an oligopoly of large operators.", "Cultural: in Norway, women's participation in RAS farm roles rose from 9% to 34% since 2015 (SDG5 Gender Equality), as roles became more technical and less 'hard-labour male-dominated'; conversely, First Nations salmon farmers in British Columbia feel disadvantaged by RAS expansion (lack of capital, reduced cultural connection, threats to native land)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "CSRD \u2014 Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [4], "facts": ["An EU reporting regulation for companies operating in the EU that came into force in 2023.", "Provides a reporting framework covering environmental, social and governance (ESG) factors.", "Presented alongside the GRI as a mechanism for 'enhancing accountability' \u2014 measuring GHG emissions is described as a precondition for organisations to develop targeted Net Zero strategies."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "GRI \u2014 Global Reporting Initiative", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [4], "facts": ["An independent organisation that has developed widely used sustainability reporting standards for businesses, covering economic, environmental and social areas.", "Its standards are aligned with intergovernmental instruments such as the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, ILO conventions, and the OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises.", "While the CSRD strengthens disclosure requirements, the GRI offers the globally recognised guidelines companies report against for transparent corporate reporting."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "UN Sustainable Development Goals Report 2024 \u2014 specific key findings", "category": "Data/Statistic", "relatedDotPoints": [5], "facts": ["SDG 1 (No Poverty): in 2022, 712 million people (about 9% of the world's population) lived in extreme poverty.", "SDG 2 (Zero Hunger): in 2023, about 733 million people faced hunger.", "SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities): in 2022, more people died during migration than in any other year in recorded history.", "Used as a task where students access the SDG Report 2024's 'Key Findings' and record one statistic against each of nine challenge categories: rising inequalities, food crisis, health setbacks, education declines, gender advancement, water/sanitation/energy access, employment, climate/biodiversity crisis, escalating conflicts, and financial resources."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "UN 2030 Agenda / UNSDGs \u2014 implementation statistics", "category": "Data/Statistic", "relatedDotPoints": [1, 3], "facts": ["The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development was adopted in 2015, building on the earlier UN Millennium Development Goals.", "Sets out an action plan around five themes: people, planet, prosperity, peace and partnership.", "169 targets provide guidance for action to integrate the four pillars of sustainability.", "193 countries have signed on; each country must complete a voluntary self-review of its progress twice between 2015 and 2030."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Cultural burning \u2014 Cape York Peninsula, Far North Queensland", "category": "Place", "relatedDotPoints": [3], "facts": ["Used as the named place-based example of Indigenous cultural burning practice in the deck (beyond the general TEK principles).", "Cultural burning supports SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and SDG 15 (Life on Land).", "Implementation requires an assessment of seasonal indicators, daily living practice, and protocols associated with kinship, including the needs of neighbouring clans and language groups; permission is sought before burning and there is no single fixed formula.", "Knowledge of Country is highly contextual (varies by location), involves complex/diverse approaches, requires understanding multi-species communities, is held and shared intergenerationally, and differs between men's and women's knowledge.", "Framed as following the precautionary principle (plus conservation and intergenerational equity principles) \u2014 maintaining cultural protocols and generating understanding of responsibility for Country."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Barcelona \u2014 anti-tourism backlash", "category": "Place", "relatedDotPoints": [9], "facts": ["Cited as an example of the 'inevitable outcome' of unsustainable tourism, where locals and the environment are 'trampled'.", "Quoted slogan used in the deck: 'If tourism doesn't work for locals, locals won't work for tourism.'", "Used alongside other visual prompts (overcrowding/erosion, pollution, litter, disrespect of locals) to have students evaluate 'Is this sustainable?' against the SEEC (Social/Economic/Environmental/Cultural) pillars."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "IHG (InterContinental Hotels Group) \u2014 hotel sustainability accreditation", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [10, 11], "facts": ["A chain-wide program used to measure and manage individual hotels' environmental impact \u2014 one of the deck's three offered 'critically analyse ONE strategy' case studies (alongside Thredbo and the transport sector).", "Individual hotels can choose from over 200 'Green Solutions' to implement.", "Operates a four-level accreditation system; hotels reaching Level 3 (L3) accreditation or above reduce energy use by 25%.", "Guests can choose 'green' accredited hotels that match their own environmental values \u2014 framed in the deck as a test of whether such programs are 'substantive or tokenistic'.", "Environmental: significant reduction in energy/water use across global hotel chains and encourages industry-wide adoption of green standards. Economic: cost savings from efficiency and a competitive advantage attracting eco-conscious travellers. Social: builds brand reputation as a sustainability leader and empowers guest choice."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Qantas \u2014 sustainable aviation strategy", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [10, 11], "facts": ["One of the deck's three offered 'critically analyse ONE strategy' case studies, representing the transport sector ('Realistic or dreaming?').", "Committed to reducing carbon emissions by 25% by 2030 (from 2019 levels) and has endorsed the aviation industry's aim of net zero emissions by 2050.", "Invests in Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) and Sustainable Marine Fuel, working with Airbus on aircraft/fuel efficiency, and partners with Greenfleet for carbon offsetting.", "Has minimised single-use plastics onboard and invests in new fuel-efficient aircraft designs.", "SAF is stated to reduce lifecycle emissions by up to 80%; offsetting mitigates unavoidable emissions, though high upfront costs for SAF/new aircraft are offset by long-term savings and brand positioning as an aviation sustainability leader. The sector's rapid growth is described as a 'daunting challenge' to sustainability efforts."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Thredbo Alpine Resort \u2014 Greenfleet offset figures (alternate stats)", "category": "Data/Statistic", "relatedDotPoints": [11], "facts": ["This version of the deck states Thredbo's Greenfleet partnership planted 46,500 native trees to offset 12,619 tonnes of carbon (slightly different figures from other course materials, which cite ~45,000 trees / 12,450 tonnes) \u2014 worth flagging/reconciling in notes.", "Achieved gold certification from EarthCheck for: powering the resort from renewable energy; closed-loop organics recycling (reduces landfill, produces compost); removal of single-use plastics; introduction of certified compostable sugarcane-pulp food packaging; and use of SNOWsat technology to map snow depth for more efficient grooming/snow-making, saving fuel and energy.", "Teaching note included in the deck: alpine areas are fragile due to harsh/variable climates, thin easily-eroded soils, steep slopes, and narrow specialised habitats for unique species \u2014 making them highly susceptible to both natural disturbance (erosion) and human impacts (development, climate change)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Sustainable/ethical tourism case studies \u2014 Elephant Nature Park, Sakha Cabs, Ccaccaccollo Women's Weaving Co-op, Quechua Homestay", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [9, 10], "facts": ["Elephant Nature Park, Chiang Mai (Thailand) \u2014 named as a sustainable/ethical tourism case study in a class documentary on tourism impacts.", "Sakha Cabs, New Delhi (India) \u2014 a taxi service cited as a sustainable tourism case study.", "Ccaccaccollo Women's Weaving Co-Op (Peru) \u2014 a community-based weaving cooperative cited as a sustainable tourism case study.", "Quechua Homestay (Peru/Andes) \u2014 a homestay-based tourism model cited as a sustainable tourism case study.", "Presented as answers to a documentary-response worksheet also covering negative impacts of tourism, the role of technology in growing global tourism, the concept and issues of 'voluntourism', and the underlying principles of sustainable tourism shown in the film."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Travelopia \u2014 horizontal integration in tourism", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [8], "facts": ["Described as the world's largest collection of specialist travel brands, illustrating horizontal integration (a company expanding by acquiring/merging with other companies at the same level of the supply chain).", "Example given: Aus Travel and American Holidays are different companies but sit at the same level (tour operators) under the Travelopia umbrella, allowing the organisation to offer a variety of products at the same stage of the distribution chain."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Virgin Group \u2014 horizontal integration in tourism", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [8], "facts": ["The Virgin Group has owned many different companies over the years with varying success.", "A key example of horizontal integration: Virgin Atlantic (UK), Virgin Australia (Australia), and the now-defunct Virgin America (USA, ceased after 2018) were all airlines operating at the same level of the distribution chain but as different companies."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "TUI Group \u2014 vertical integration in tourism", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [8], "facts": ["One of the biggest travel companies in the world; since the collapse of Thomas Cook, TUI holds a near-monopoly on tour operator operations in the UK and other parts of the world.", "Owns companies at both the same level (horizontal integration) and different levels (vertical integration) of the tourism distribution chain \u2014 including tour operations, travel agents, an airline, and in-destination services.", "Used as the deck's core example of vertical integration: a company expanding by acquiring businesses at different stages of the supply chain (e.g. a tour operator buying an airline and hotel chain, or an airline buying a travel agency)."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Internationalism in tourism \u2014 UNESCO World Heritage Sites & Australia\u2013China Class C visas", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [3, 8], "facts": ["UNWTO cited as promoting sustainable tourism globally by encouraging countries to collaborate on policy, share best practice, and develop international standards.", "International travel agreements example: Class C visas between Australia and China, allowing freer movement for tourists across borders.", "UNESCO's World Heritage Sites program is cited as protecting culturally and naturally significant places worldwide for international visitors \u2014 an example of 'internationalism' as a current trend shaping global tourism."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Butler Model (Tourism Area Life Cycle) \u2014 'victims of their own success'", "category": "Concept/Term", "relatedDotPoints": [9], "facts": ["Introduced in the deck under the heading 'Victims of their own success \u2013 The Butler Model', used to frame the question of whether/how tourism can become unsustainable over time as destinations develop.", "Directly precedes a sequence of image-based prompts ('Is this sustainable? SEEC?') illustrating overcrowding/erosion, pollution, litter, and disrespect of locals as symptoms of destinations succeeding past their sustainable capacity."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "World Tourism Barometer 2016 \u2014 global tourist arrivals data", "category": "Data/Statistic", "relatedDotPoints": [6], "facts": ["International tourist arrivals (overnight visitors) reached 1,186 million in 2015, an increase of 52 million over the previous year.", "Three major factors influenced 2015 tourism flows: unusually strong exchange rate fluctuations, declining oil/commodity prices (raising disposable income in importing countries but weakening demand in exporting countries), and increased global concern about safety and security.", "By UNWTO region: the Americas and Asia-Pacific each recorded close to 6% growth in arrivals; Europe recorded 5%; the Middle East increased by 2%; Africa saw an estimated 3% decline (driven by weak results in North Africa).", "By subregion, the strongest growth was in South-East Asia (+8%), followed by Oceania, the Caribbean, Central America and Northern Europe (all +7%), then North America and South America (both +6%).", "Quoted directly from the World Tourism Barometer 2016 report."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}, {"name": "Sustainable tourism accreditation bodies \u2014 ATIC, GSTC, and MNC quality control (Hilton, Disney)", "category": "Organisation/Initiative", "relatedDotPoints": [7], "facts": ["Australian Tourism Industry Council (ATIC) and Global Sustainable Tourism Council (GSTC) are cited as organisational bodies providing sustainable tourism accreditation.", "Globalised multinational companies such as Hilton and Disney are cited as ensuring quality control across their tourism operations worldwide.", "Political influence example: Tourism Australia promotes destinations, while 'smartraveller.gov.au' provides government travel advisory support \u2014 both cited as organisational/political influences (the 'O' and 'P' in BETOP) on global tourism."], "source": "Unit 1 teaching materials"}];